Arkansas’ trip to the Bahamas offered an early glimpse of what the Razorbacks could look like this season, and few players made a stronger impression than freshman JJ Andrews. From the opening game to the finale, Andrews emerged as one of Arkansas’ most impressive performers, flashing the athleticism and physicality that stood out throughout the week.

The freshman averaged 18.0 points and 7.5 rebounds, scoring in double figures in all four contests while helping the Razorbacks complete a 4-0 exhibition trip. Andrews attacked the basket with confidence, finished through contact, and competed on the glass. His motor was just as noticeable as his scoring, whether it was battling for rebounds, chasing down loose balls, or making plays on the defensive end.

His best performance came in the finale against the University of Calgary, where he finished with 24 points, six rebounds, four steals, and two blocks while shooting 10-of-13 from the field and 3-of-4 from three-point range. He also posted a 15-point, 13-rebound double-double against Toros del Valle, showing he can affect the game as a rebounder just as much as a scorer.

Four exhibition games won’t define Andrews’ freshman season, but they reinforced why he’s expected to play a meaningful role this year. His production, physicality, and approach made him one of the biggest takeaways from Arkansas’ undefeated trip and an early bright spot heading into the regular season.

